Cloverleaf Raga
Ingredients: FOLK ROCK + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Drone + 1960s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Born when anti-freeway dissidents spliced 12-string acoustic guitars and reed harmoniums directly into municipal traffic light relays to broadcast hypnotic drones through civil defense horn speakers.

Cloverleaf Raga is a hypnotic, sun-bleached collision of 1960s British-American folk-rock jangle and sprawling drone mysticism. Pastoral fingerpicking, open-tuned 12-string acoustics, and communal vocal harmonies are anchored not by a standard rhythm section, but by the endless low-frequency hum of mains electricity, bowed dulcimers, and the rhythmic, metronomic clacking of electromechanical relay switches.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 68–92 BPM; rhythmic movement is floating, rubato, and trance-like, pushed forward by hand-beaten tambourines, walking thumb-bass acoustic patterns, and the motorized, synchronous tick of automated roadside relays rather than conventional drum kits.
- Harmonic Palette: Open modal tunings (DADGAD, Open C, and Highland pipe drones), Mixolydian and Dorian modes, root-fifth parallel intervals, and microtonal string bends emulating sitar and tanpura over a static root note.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Transformer Induction Capture: Direct inductive pickup coils taped against steel electrical utility boxes to feed continuous 60Hz hum and switching transients directly into acoustic guitar preamps.
  2. Twin-Reel Flanging: Duplicated reel-to-reel tape loops of bowed instruments run slightly out of phase with manual tape-flange braking, creating thick, psychedelic comb-filter sweeps.
  3. Spring Reverb Caverns: Massive analog spring tanks driven hard into mild Germanium tube breakup, dissolving crystalline guitar strums into luminous, continuous acoustic pads.
- Dynamics: Arc-form builds; songs open with a singular, quiet fingerpicked figure over a buzzing ground tone, slowly gathering layers of harmonium breath, bowed strings, and unison chanting into a blinding, ecstatic peak before falling back into raw mechanical clicking.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement flared to life in the late summer of 1967 along the contested highway corridors of California's Central Coast, where the state began clearing ancient oak groves to expand four-lane bypasses. A collective of Berkeley dropouts, pacifist audio engineers, and traveling folk musicians known as the "Diggers of the Interchange" broke into electromechanical junction boxes at construction checkpoints. Armed with patched telephone transceivers, contact mics, and battery-powered portable amplifiers, they turned the infrastructure into an acoustic amplifier, blanketing bulldozed clearings in undulating raga drones that confused work crews and halted morning shifts. The scene soon developed its own ritual culture: Sunday "shoulder sit-ins" where audiences clad in oil-smudged linen tunics, work jackets, and dried laurel wreaths shared communal pots of herbal tea and seeded bread while lingering under concrete overpasses, treating the acoustic reverberation of the freeway undercroft as a cathedral of resistance.

Typical Instruments
- Acoustic Guitars: 12-string Guild and Martin flat-tops tuned to DADGAD and Open D.
- Drone & Folk Instruments: Foot-pumped field harmonium, shruti box, bowed Appalachian mountain dulcimer, and wooden transverse flutes.
- Hacked Infrastructure: Inductive telephone taps, contact-mic'd solenoid switches, and electromechanical traffic signal timers.
- Electronics & Amplification: Maestro Echoplex EP-2 tape delay, vintage tube spring reverb tanks, and Germanium transistor boost pedals.
- Percussion: Rim-tapped tambourines, seed shakers, finger cymbals, and bodhrán.

For Fans Of: Sandy Bull, Pentangle, Robbie Basho, early Fairport Convention, Spacemen 3.

