- Story & Context: Silas Finch used a flathead screwdriver and a kitchen butter knife to pry open the sequencing box at the Highway 101 interchange just as county bulldozers crested the hill. As steam hissed from a copper camping kettle boiling over an open camp stove nearby, Silas jammed a telephone pickup into the high-voltage relay, sending a roaring acoustic 12-string raga through the civil defense horns that made the grading crews drop their shovels in awe. Through the squealing squawk of overdriven gantry speakers, he howled the rallying cry, "The pot won't boil until the highway halts," while two hundred demonstrators sat down in the wet gravel. The state troopers were forced to shut down the machines until sundown, entirely baffled by the folk song pulsing out of their own signal towers.
